  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Fri Jul 3 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will start to move off to the east\nSaturday night into Sunday with and upper level northwest flow\npattern setting up west of the ridge across the region. \nAdditional storms are expected to develop across portions of \ncentral Kansas Saturday evening where uncapped MUCAPE will be \nin place. The MUCAPE will spread east into Missouri Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. The storms across Kansas will likely \ndevelop into a complex again and push southeast Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning. The better upper level shear will be north \nof the area, but a few strong storms with gusty winds will be \npossible with this activity. The exact track of this activity \nwill be dependent on exactly with the complex develops, if it \ndevelops further south and west it could miss much of the area, \nbut if a complex of storms develop further north and east \nbetter chances for portions of the area being impacted would \noccur. Scattered afternoon/early evening storms will be possible\nagain on Sunday.\n\nAnother upper level ridge will build towards the plains early\nnext week and over the plain by the middle of next the week\nremaining west of the area. This will leave the area in a \nnorthwest upper level flow pattern much of next week. There \nwill be the potential for thunderstorms to develop to the \nnorthwest of the area and move southeast towards the area during\nthe overnight/morning hours. Confidences in exactly where \nstorms will develop and track is still low at this time, but \nwith the area being in a northwest flow upper level pattern\nportions of the area will have the potential of being impacted. \nIsolated/scattered afternoon/early evening storms could be \npossible each day next week too, especially if outflow \nboundaries form overnight convection can stall over the area. \n\nTemperatures will return to closer to normal next week with\nhighs in the upper 80s to the lower 90s with heat index values \ngenerally in the lower 90s to near 100 degrees each afternoon \nnext week.",
